Date: Mon, 5 Apr 1993 20:25:31 -0400 (EDT)
From: Carol Moore <cmoore@cap.gwu.edu>
Subject: PPL: Scope of/Description of Providers

Two comments/questions, one about the scope of protection/
legal/insurance services offered by firms under privately
produced/polycentric law and and the other about what to
call these service providers, especially the full service
providers.  
  
At first glance it seems it would be most effective and cost
efficient to have the bulk of them provided by one company,
hiring additional ones only if they specialize in some
unusual area.  However, variations in expertise, the
possibility of conflicts of interest always arise, etc.
might suggest it is better to buy services from only two of
the three providers--or even only one service--from any one
firm.
  
Any thoughts on these concrete mechanics of PPL?   
  
Also, I've been thinking about what is the best name for
both describing and *promoting to the public* such
protection/legal/insurance services, especially full service
providers, since providers of only one or the other would
probably use existing names.  I personally haven't come up
with one I'm totally excited about.
  
Below are some existing or possible descriptive generic
names for such services, be they separate or combined. 
(Plus comments on a couple of them.) I'm curious about what
other appellations have been/are being used and which names
other posters prefer.
  
INSURANCE ONLY
insurance
surety
assurance
warranty
guarantee
  
SECURITY ONLY
private security agencies/services
security guard and patrol agencies/services
personal protection agencies/services
security consultant
  
LEGAL ONLY
attorney
lawyer
law office
arbitrator
mediator
legal services provider
general practice
  
COMBINED SERVICES
private protection agencies/services 
 
protection agencies/services (first two seem to be most
   popular, however, critics might complain it sounds
   like "an organized crime protection racket".)
full service providers (too vague)
legal/protection agencies/services (too clunky)
legal security agencies/services (sounds good but. . .)
free market defense agencies/services (too ideological)
  
Some things to think about as we prepare to offer our
alternative to the world!






