Date: Mon, 5 Apr 93 17:45:39 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Reputation Markets--I'm Confused

I just reread Brian Hawthorne's proposal (HEX: Introducing the Hawthorne
Exchange, 3-24-93) to figure out the point of it. 

* Who or what makes the market? If the "price" of Peter Poster is, say,
"100 Thornes," and is listed as such, how does it ever go up or down?

In normal markets there is some kind of bidding process, exemplified by a
range between "bid" and "asked" prices, or more concretely by haggling, as
in "I'll tell you what--I'll pay 96 Thornes apiece for 10 shares of Peter
Poster." Thusly, the price can move up or down.

Or, when the shares of Peter Poster are not selling at 100 Thornes, sellers
can reduce their offering price. I hope the software Brian is working on
will offer this richness of interaction.

I haven't seen discussion of how a market price will be established. (The
issue of whether it's worth anybody's time to trade is another issue
entirely.)

* What's the "underlying market value" of the commodities traded? I know, I
know, its "reputation." But it looks just like a *popularity contest*.
Reputations _can_ mean this, but more often they mean something more, e.g.,
that the words of Alice are taken more seriously than the words of Bob
(that real decisions hinge on the reputations, or that people buy the
output (books, art, postings) of Alice over Bob).

I understand that markets need not trade in tangibles, that markets can
take on a reality unto themselves (as in the Dutch tulip bulb speculative
bubble), but this market seems generally uninteresting.

* I have no interest in seeing a popularity poll on my postings. I value
the postings of some people and I am interested in _their_ reactions to my
postings, mostly as expressed via their responses. A vote of the herd is
boring.

Just my opinions, of course.

-Tim May
 
