Date: Mon, 5 Apr 93 19:48:58 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

Lefty writes:

> Heh.  If we define intelligence as the amount of time it takes one to tie
> one's shoelaces, we can dramatically increase our intelligence by buying
> loafers.

I have to agree with the general criticism of intelligence tests.
In fact, I'm rather dubious of any project that wants to define
"intelligence" in a measurable way -- what's the point?  If person
A has a score of 150 and person B has a score of 120 on some sort
of intelligence scale, presumably A has 25% more of something than
B.  What would that be?

If forced to define intelligence I'd probably say something like
"the ability to clearly define, and then achieve, personal goals" --
but that's a great deal broader than the rather peculiar mental
tricks that tests like the MondoBrain or Stanford-bidet measure.
What's interesting to me is that there's such a weak correlation
between abilities measured by A::B as C::? questions and active
life mastery.  The defenders of IQ as a concept might like to wish
that some kind of equation like "intelligence + will = accomplishment"
holds, but I suspect that there is a great deal more dependence on the
specific domain of accomplishment under consideration than most
Mensans would be happy to admit.

That said, if I remember the kinds of questions asked on IQ tests,
some real-world domains (such as, say, mechanical engineering and
maybe computer science and some aspects of law) use as basic tools
things that look very much like that (excluding the perhaps most
important parts of those and other fields -- such as communication
ability, "creativity", drive, ...).

Anyway, having said that, there are some ways that I'd like my
intelligence enhanced (though I doubt that exercises would help
very much) -- it'd be nice if I could have a much larger short-
term memory capacity, a much more comprehensive and accurate
long-term memory, better pattern-recognition skills, the ability
to effectively multi-task at complex tasks, and so on.

