Date: Mon, 5 Apr 93 18:11:31 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

> But perceptual systems that depend on specific time delays probably
> have self-compensating mechanisms to adjust to variable conduction
> speed.  (Or at least, they self-compensate during development.)

For perceptual systems, what probably happens (assuming the
dendrite-delay sensors exist in actual wetware, for which there is
no evidence of which I am aware) is that you have a whole bunch that
happen to be tuned for different speeds, and the ones that do
nothing useful die.

> The sort of symbolic thought that IQ tests and the like measure *do*
> seem to consist of serial actions, which might depend on raw nerve
> speed.  Have you ever said to someone "I'm tired/drunk, and so I'm
> thinking slowly right now?"

Yes, but what does idiom have to do with ion channels?
"Serial actions", in any case, are "serial" only in that they look
serial to a cognitive psychologist.  They are not performed serially
in the brain; it has no way to do such a thing.  To expand upon my
point that altering transmission speed will hose things, consider
the following arrangement:

            |
------O--+-<| (excitatory synapse)
  _______|  |
 |_______   |
         |  |
         +-<| (inhibitory synapse)
            |
      +-----|------______
      |     O       v
    --+     |       |---
	    |
            +-----> off elsewhence

When the top cell fires, it will start up a post-synaptic potential
at the exitatory synapse.  The post-synaptic side will still be high
by the time the action potential gets to the inhibitory synapse,
which shorts out the dendrite for longer than the duration of the
PSP, thus clipping it off short.  If transmission were faster, the
inhibitory synapse will fire immediately after the excitatory,
preventing any signal from reaching the bottom cell body at all.
We can assume that this would be bad.

Hmm, actually I think clipping may be done in a much smaller-scale
arrangement, but we can generally postulate that any circuit
involving feedback paths, or having a transmission delay
interrelated to non-transmission delays, will have problems with
just changing the clock crystal.

