Subject: Tailoring Memes to Win Envirnmentalists

   Todays environmentalists insist that we preserve ecosystems to preserve
diversity and that man should not interfere in ecosystems too much. In
most cases, these people are extremely totalitarian and socialist
in nature. Question: How to win them over? Answer: We should try as
hard as possible to tailor our memes so that they get past the natural
barriers people put up. Once the subject is "hooked" to use fishing
terminology, reeling them is the easy part.

  I have looked for ways to sneak extropian memes in, and I think I have
found a fairly obvious one -- the resemblance of a market to an ecosystem.

   The free-market is an ecosystem in its own right constantly producing
variations on its products to yield a high diversity. One example is
the soap industry where a multitude of brand-names and types differ only
by small characteristics such as scent, color, or packaging. No centrally
controlled economy has produced the diversity of products that markets have
and there is no reason they should. 
   Centrally controlling an market will reduce total diversity and
prevent new diversity from appearing. Controlling a market
would be analogous to controlling the gene pool of an ecosystem --
an act that many environmentalists would consider blasphemous. So if you
accept that ecosystems and diversity in biology should be protected, it
seems only natural that markets should also be protected from interference.

  Governments which interfere in markets are by their very nature
"exploiting" the ecosystem of the market and thus are immoral.
(if target is sufficiently crackpotish enough, include rambling about
how bureaucrats are analogous to cancer)

-Ray

ps. You can now add biology and computer science to the list of
justifications of capitalism over socialism.

(the computer science comes in by applying genetic algorithm reasoning to
the market to show that the market is more efficient at selecting solutions
[because the solution space is large] than a linear government approach.)


