Ah, this cuts to the issue at hand!

If I want to buy an ounce of gold, it doesn't matter what price I think it
"should" be, it only matters what others will sell it to me for. (Nitpicky
point: if I think $330 an ounce is too high, and don't buy, this will have
some tiny effect on the price, perhaps moving it down a microcent.)

The problem with the HEx is that nothing is being bought or sold. I'm not
likely to use the HEx system for a simple reason: *it doesn't reflect my
own sense of values*. It's a popularity contest.

I can't use it for my own ranking of "to be reads" and "to be discardeds"
unless I have the same ranking preferences as the herd. (The contrast with
a market in gold or books or whatever is that I am actually buying
something from others, so it doesn't matter that my ranking system doesn't
match the overall herd's.)

